// @polsia:user-owned — in-app messaging models.
//
// Linked to better-auth's `User` by SCALAR `userAId` / `userBId` / `senderId`
// columns only — NEVER a Prisma `@relation` back to `User`. `auth.prisma` is
// framework-owned (the better-auth AGENT.md forbids editing it to add a back-
// relation field); all FK-style references live as scalar ids and we read the
// `User` / `Profile` rows in app code by id.
//
// Canonical-pair invariant — the SAME two users always map to ONE
// `MessageThread` row. The `@@unique([userAId, userBId])` index dedupes
// concurrent writes, but only because every writer sorts its pair into a
// deterministic order before insert. Implementers of a future
// `POST /api/messages/threads` MUST do:
//     const [a, b] = [userA, userB].sort();
//     await prisma.messageThread.upsert({
//       where: { userAId_userBId: { userAId: a, userBId: b } },
//       create: { userAId: a, userBId: b, lastMessageAt: new Date() },
//       update: { lastMessageAt: new Date() },
//     });
// so any participant can list "their" conversations with a single
// `WHERE userAId = ? OR userBId = ?` and never see two rows for the same
// chat. Without that sort the index is NOT enough — two writers racing from
// opposite sides can both reach `create()` and end up with two threads
// (the unique index would then reject whichever loses the race, masking the
// bug rather than fixing the conversation list).
//
// Both `@@index([userAId, lastMessageAt(desc)])` and
// `@@index([userBId, lastMessageAt(desc)])` exist so the listing query
// `WHERE userAId = ? OR userBId = ? ORDER BY lastMessageAt DESC` uses an
// index on either side without forcing a sort.
//
// Datasource + generator live in `prisma/schema/_base.prisma` — do NOT
// redeclare them here.

model MessageThread {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  userAId       String
  userBId       String
  // Bumped on every new Message (and on the upsert that initially opens the
  // thread). Denormalized so the listing endpoint doesn't have to JOIN to
  // the most recent Message per thread — critical because the scan we care
  // about is "my conversations ordered by recency", not "all messages".
  lastMessageAt DateTime
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())

  @@unique([userAId, userBId])
  @@index([userAId, lastMessageAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@index([userBId, lastMessageAt(sort: Desc)])
}

model Message {
  id        String    @id @default(cuid())
  threadId  String
  senderId  String
  body      String
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  // Null until the recipient opens the thread. Set by a future
  // PATCH /api/messages/threads/:id/read write; out of scope for this turn.
  readAt    DateTime?

  // Drives the "last message preview" lookup per thread — list per-thread
  // newest-first, take the first row per threadId.
  @@index([threadId, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@index([senderId, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
}
